diff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md
index c7e30614..2491b92c 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -40,6 +40,7 @@

 ### Bug Fixes

+- [Bug Fix] **`~/.coi` in the container belongs to the code user** — when the container had no `~/.coi`, `coi run --prompt` and `coi tool spec` created it as root (only `~/.coi/runs` was handed to the code user), so the code user could not write there. coi now creates a missing `~/.coi` owned by the code user. An existing `~/.coi` keeps its owner.
 - [Bug Fix] **`coi file push -r` to another name keeps the owner of files coi does not own** — pushing a directory under a new name sent a copy made on the host, and a non-root coi cannot give the copy another owner. Root-owned (or other users') files and directories came out owned by your user in the container. With Incus 7.0 or later, coi now pushes the source tree itself, so every entry keeps its owner, and nothing is copied. With Incus 6.x, or when the tree holds a FIFO, socket or device (which coi skips), coi still sends a copy, so such entries still come out owned by your user.
 - [Bug Fix] **Protected paths with characters such as `@` or `:` are protected on every Incus version** — a `[security] protected_paths` or `additional_protected_paths` entry whose device name Incus rejected made the mount fail, so coi only printed a warning, and that path and the protected paths after it were not mounted read-only. Affected: a path with a character other than letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `-` and `/` (e.g. `node_modules/@scope/pkg`; Incus 7.6 rejects all of them, older versions some, such as a space or `+`), a long path, or, on Incus 7.0+, one ending with `_` or `-` (e.g. `__pycache__`). coi now gives such paths a hashed device name.
 - [Bug Fix] **The allowlist session log no longer says the LAN is blocked when `allow_local_network_access = true`** — `coi logs` showed "Blocking all RFC1918 private networks" and "Blocking cloud metadata endpoints" in `allowlist` mode even when local network access was on and the LAN was reachable. It now says that RFC1918 private networks are allowed, and on which ports when `allowed_ports` is set.
